The dispersion stability was also quantied for selected examples through PXRD analysis of the upper layer of the suspension. The ratio of crystalline (solid) to amorphous (oil) content was determined by a known application of Rietveld renement and monitored for up to nine days (Fig. 2b and ESI $5\dagger.$ ).10 Given the large number of mixtures prepared, only a selection (specically Al(fum)(OH)/brominated vegetable oil, $\mathbf{Al(fum)(OH)/PDMS}$ and ZIF-8/PDMS) were analysed by this method. Fig. 2b shows data for these three dispersions, each showing different behaviour, specically low stability due to otation of the solid (Al(fum)(OH)/brominated vegetable oil), good stability (ZIF-8/PDMS) and low stability indicated by sedimentation (Al(fum)(OH)/PDMS).
## Gas solubilities
Having formed a range of dispersions, some with stabilities of days to weeks, a critical next question is whether the pores of the dispersed solids are still empty and available to guests, i.e. whether the molecules of the liquids do, as hoped, remain outside the pores. To probe this, we screened the solubilities of gases in these PLs using an isochoric method described elsewhere.11 Initially $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ solubilities at ca. 0.8 bar in the putative porous liquids were rst measured and compared to the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ solubilities in their pure liquid components (Fig. 3a–e; details in ESI 6†). A minority of the dispersions (e.g. HKUST-1/paraffin, ZIF-8/Genosorb $\textsuperscript{\textregistered}$ 1753) showed the same or even lower $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ uptake than that observed for their neat liquid component (i.e. there was no enhancement of gas uptake due to the presence of the porous solid). This occurred particularly where non-bulky liquid molecules were used and we attribute this to the ability of those liquids to penetrate into the pores of the solid, but it is also possible, in principle, that coordinating species such as PEG-type polymers block gas inclusion by adhering strongly to the external surfaces of the solid particles. Supporting the inclusion hypothesis, PEG chains with bulky end groups (i.e. benzoate, acrylate and ethylhexanoate) did give dispersions with enhanced $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ uptake (for example, see entries for ZIF-8 in Table 2). Remarkably, for most of the dispersions prepared (Table 1) the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ solubility was increased by a factor of 3–6 compared to their neat liquid components $\mathrm{\left(ESI\6\dagger^{\cdot}\right)}$ ). Illustrative examples include HKUST-1/PDMS and HKUST-1/olive oil (Fig. 3). As described in the Rationale above, in most cases this can be correlated with intuitive steric effects; the bulky $\mathrm{sime}_{3}$ end groups of PDMS and the branched core structure of olive oil (a triglyceride), sterically prevented them from entering the pores of MOF, leaving the pores accessible to gases. However, at least one instance of high gas uptake are surprising. In particular, PAF-1 has large pores (pore size distribution centred around $1.4\ \mathrm{nm}$ ),12a,12b which would be expected to be large enough for non-bulky linear polymers such as Genosorb® 1753 to diffuse into. However, the dispersion PAF-1/Genosorb $\textsuperscript{\textregistered}$ 1753 shows signicantly enhanced $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ uptake (0.72 mmol $\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ ) compared to the pure liquid $\left(0.23\mathrm{\mmol\g}^{-1}\right)$ , suggesting that the full porosity of PAF-1 remains accessible when dispersed in this medium (ESI 6†) (note that MOFs with smaller pores such as HKUST-1 $\left(0.9\ \mathrm{nm}\right)$ , and $\mathbf{Al(fum)OH}$ $\left(0.6\ \mathrm{nm}\right)$ do appear to become occupied by Genosorb $\textsuperscript{\textregistered}$ 1753 (Table 2)).
In the above studies the amount of porous solid used was arbitrarily chosen to be $12.5~\mathrm{wt\%}$ . However, we noted that for some of the most stable dispersions, such as HKUST-1/olive oil, it was possible to increase this to $25~\mathrm{wt\%}$ without noticeable sedimentation.
Comparison of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ uptake using silicone oils of different viscosities revealed that there was no difference in the amount of gas uptake, but that the rate of uptake was slower for the more viscous oil as expected $\left(\mathrm{ESI}\ 7\dagger\right)$ ).13
